+# BLOCKED: exact streaming median in constant memory
+
+## Requirement
+
+`test_constant_memory` requires `median()` to compute the **exact** median of an
+arbitrary 200,000-element stream of floats while using **O(1) memory** (peak under
+64 KB), and `median()` must still accept a one-shot iterable/generator.
+
+## Why this is impossible
+
+An exact median of arbitrary data cannot be computed in constant memory.
+
+- The median is an order statistic. To know the value separating the lower and
+  upper halves, an algorithm must retain enough information to identify the
+  element at rank `n // 2` (or the two elements at ranks `n//2 - 1` and `n//2`).
+- A constant-memory streaming algorithm has only a fixed number of states. But
+  the input can be in any of `n!` orderings, and for distinct inputs the median
+  can be any of the `n` values. Distinguishing those outcomes while reading the
+  stream once requires at least `Ω(n)` bits of state (in the comparison/streaming
+  model, selection has an `Ω(n)` space lower bound for exact answers when the
+  input cannot be revisited and values are arbitrary).
+- Concretely for this test, every one of the 200,000 `float` values may be the
+  median. A constant-memory implementation must discard some inputs, and an
+  adversary can place the true median among the discarded values. Any bound well
+  below 64 KB is far smaller than the ~1.6 MB needed just to hold 200k 8-byte
+  floats, let alone the more than that Python objects require.
+- Approximate streaming algorithms (P², t-digest, reservoir sampling, fixed-size
+  histograms) use bounded memory but do not produce the exact value. The test
+  compares with `assertEqual`, so approximation fails.
+- `tracemalloc` measures allocations inside `median()`, so we also cannot hide an
+  O(n) buffer outside the traced region or pre-read the generator without
+  violating the spirit and the generator-input requirement.
+
+## What would unblock this
+
+Choose one of:
+
+1. **Relax exactness**: accept an approximate quantile (e.g. P² or t-digest)
+   with a documented error bound, and update the test to compare within tolerance.
+2. **Relax the memory bound**: allow O(n) memory (the current `sorted()` approach)
+   or an on-disk/external-memory selection algorithm.
+3. **Change the input contract**: allow multiple passes over a rewindable source
+   or values from a known small domain, enabling counting/bucket selection.
+
+## What was done instead
+
+- Fixed the reported correctness bug: for an even number of values `median()` now
+  returns the mean of the two middle values (`median([1, 2, 3, 4]) == 2.5`).
+- Left `median()` exact and O(n) in memory, because that is the only way to satisfy
+  the exactness and one-shot-iterable requirements.
+- `test_constant_memory` therefore still fails on the memory bound (and its exact
+  value check), by design; the other four tests pass.
